## Proof

**Setup.** Place $S$ at the origin. Since $A_1$ lies on ray $SA_0$ beyond $A_0$, write $A_1 = kA_0$ for some $k > 1$. The condition $A_1B_1 \parallel A_0B_0$ with $B_1$ on ray $SB_0$ forces $B_1 = kB_0$ (same ratio), since $B_1 - A_1 = k(B_0 - A_0)$ requires both coefficients equal. Write $A_i = a_i A_0$, $B_i = b_i B_0$ with $a_0 = b_0 = 1$, $a_1 = b_1 = k$.

**Symmetry.** The definitions of $A_i$ and $B_i$ are swapped under $A \leftrightarrow B$. Since $a_0 = b_0$ and $a_1 = b_1$, by induction $a_i = b_i$ for all $i$. Thus $M_i = \frac{a_i(A_0 + B_0)}{2}$ and
$$SM_i = \frac{|a_i|}{2}|A_0 + B_0|, \qquad \frac{SM_{i-1}}{SM_i} = \frac{|a_{i-1}|}{|a_i|} = \frac{1}{|r_i|}$$
where $r_i := a_i / a_{i-1}$.

**Recursion.** The line $B_{i-2}M_{i-1}$ meets line $SA_0$ (which contains $A_{i-2}, A_{i-1}$) at $A_i$. Computing the $B_0$-component to vanish yields the parameter $t = \frac{2a_{i-2}}{2a_{i-2} - a_{i-1}}$ along $B_{i-2}M_{i-1}$, giving
$$a_i = \frac{a_{i-2}\,a_{i-1}}{2a_{i-2} - a_{i-1}}, \qquad r_i = \frac{a_i}{a_{i-1}} = \frac{1}{2 - r_{i-1}}, \quad r_1 = k.$$

**Ray condition.** The intersection lies on **both rays** $B_{i-2}M_{i-1}^\to$ and $A_{i-2}A_{i-1}^\to$ iff the parameter $t = \frac{2}{2 - r_{i-1}} \ge 0$, i.e., iff $r_{i-1} < 2$. (The same condition governs both rays.) When $r_{i-1} \ge 2$, the intersection does not exist and $M_i$ is undefined.

**Solving the Möbius iteration.** Set $s_i := \frac{1}{r_i - 1}$. Then:
$$r_i - 1 = \frac{r_{i-1}-1}{2-r_{i-1}}, \qquad s_i = \frac{2-r_{i-1}}{r_{i-1}-1} = -1 + \frac{1}{r_{i-1}-1} = s_{i-1} - 1.$$
So $s_i = s_1 - (i-1) = \frac{1}{k-1} - (i-1)$, and
$$r_i = 1 + \frac{k-1}{1 - (k-1)(i-1)}.$$

**Termination.** Let $c = k - 1 > 0$ and $n = \lceil 1/c \rceil$. Then $n \ge 1/c$, i.e., $cn \ge 1$, and $n < 1/c + 1$. For $r_n$:
$$r_n \ge 2 \iff \frac{c}{1 - c(n-1)} \ge 1 \iff cn \ge 1 \iff n \ge \frac{1}{c}. \checkmark$$
(The denominator $1 - c(n-1) > 0$ since $n - 1 < 1/c$.) So $r_n \ge 2$, meaning $M_{n+1}$ **does not exist**.

For all $j < n$: since $j \le n - 1 < 1/c$, we get $cj < 1$, so $r_j < 2$ and $M_{j+1}$ exists. Once $M_{n+1}$ fails to exist, all subsequent $M_i$ ($i \ge n+1$) are also undefined (their construction requires $M_{n+1}$), so every term $\frac{SM_{i-1}}{SM_i}$ for $i \ge n+1$ equals $0$.

**Conclusion.** The series $\sum_{i \ge 2} \frac{SM_{i-1}}{SM_i}$ has at most $n - 1 = \lceil \frac{1}{k-1} \rceil - 1$ nonzero terms (for $i = 2, \ldots, n$), each equal to $\frac{1}{|r_i|}$, and all remaining terms are $0$. Being a **finite sum**, the series **converges** for every valid configuration ($k > 1$). $\blacksquare$

